WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:08.400
[Your purchase of Christmas Seals]

00:00:09.200 --> 00:00:12.000
[Helps Fight TB All Year Long!]

00:00:20.800 --> 00:00:25.400
[Animated billboard reading 
Your Tuberculosis Association]

00:00:25.800 --> 00:00:27.800
[Presents]

00:00:28.200 --> 00:00:34.200
["Rodney"]

00:00:35.200 --> 00:00:38.200
[Designed and Directed by Lu Guarnier.]

00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:41.400
[Animation:
Don Towsley, Cliff Augustson, Preston Blair, Robert Soria.]

00:00:41.600 --> 00:00:44.600
[Story:
E.R. Murkland - Stan Russell]

00:00:44.800 --> 00:00:47.800
[Backgrounds:
Herman Olinsky, David Salter, Van Kaufman, Bud Simpson.]

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:51.000
[Music: Jack Shaindlin.]

00:00:51.200 --> 00:00:54.200
Camera: C.J. Foster]

00:00:56.000 --> 00:01:04.000
[Produced by Film Graphics Inc. 
Supervised by Film Counselors, Inc.]

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:11.000
[Music]

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:15.600
[Narrator]: It's not the biggest town in
the state and it's not the smallest.

00:01:15.800 --> 00:01:21.000
It's an average town
and people here are pretty much like people anywhere.

00:01:21.400 --> 00:01:24.030
For instance, take Rodney

00:01:24.200 --> 00:01:28.600
[Rodney strides down the street confidently,
past storefronts and his fellow townspeople.]

00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:33.000
[Narrator:] He's courteous and he's friendly.

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:36.600
[Guy sitting on steps:] Hey, here comes Rod.
Hi ya, Rod.

00:01:36.600 --> 00:01:37.500
[Rodney:] Hi ya.

00:01:38.510 --> 00:01:40.050
[Narrator:] He's popular too.

00:01:40.050 --> 00:01:41.800
[Girl standing on sidewalk:] Hello, Rodney.

00:01:41.900 --> 00:01:42.400
[Rodney:] Hi ya.

00:01:42.400 --> 00:01:46.100
[Music]

00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:47.400
[Narrator:] He's good at sports.

00:01:47.500 --> 00:01:54.000
[Rodney rushes down the football field,
ball tucked under his arm.]

00:01:55.000 --> 00:01:56.200
[Narrator:] He's law-abiding.

00:01:56.400 --> 00:02:01.500
[A policeman blows his whistle while holding his hand out. 
Rodney stops on the street corner.]

00:02:02.400 --> 00:02:04.200
[Narrator:] And he's helpful.

00:02:04.500 --> 00:02:10.200
[Rodney helps an elderly woman carry a stack
of packages across the street.]

00:02:12.400 --> 00:02:15.800
[Narrator:] And Rodney's smart enough to
get a physical checkup once a year,

00:02:15.800 --> 00:02:23.400
[The doctor listens to Rodney's heart, checks his height, weight, temperature, and blood pressure.]

00:02:23.600 --> 00:02:25.800
 including a chest x-ray.

00:02:27.400 --> 00:02:34.000
[A spinning x-ray film with background music]

00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:37.000
[The doctor and Rodney stand together examing
X-ray film propped on a table.]

00:02:37.100 --> 00:02:38.800
[Rodney:] Gee doctor, is that what I look like?

00:02:39.800 --> 00:02:40.600
[Doctor:] Hmmm.

00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:41.830
[Rodney:] What's the matter?

00:02:42.100 --> 00:02:46.000
[Doctor:] Seems to be a spot here, son.

00:02:46.100 --> 00:02:48.000
[Rodney:] A spot, where, in me?

00:02:48.400 --> 00:02:53.700
[Doctor:] Here in your lobe. But we can't
tell whether it means anything just from this x-ray. 

00:02:54.600 --> 00:02:57.800
We'll have to make some other tests such as a tuberculin test.

00:02:57.800 --> 00:02:58.800
[Rodney:] Tuberculin test?

00:02:58.800 --> 00:03:00.690
[Doctor:] And we'll get another x-ray.

00:03:00.690 --> 00:03:03.300
[Rodney:] Another x-ray? 
What's it all about? Am I sick?

00:03:03.300 --> 00:03:09.300
[Music]

00:03:09.300 --> 00:03:16.400
[Doctor:] Well, Rodney, the laboratory test
and this new x-ray show that you have active TB, 

00:03:16.800 --> 00:03:17.800
tuberculosis.]

00:03:19.200 --> 00:03:24.800
[Rodney:] Tuberculosis, me? You must be wrong, doc.
I've never felt better in my life.

00:03:25.200 --> 00:03:27.600
[Doctor:] Early TB doesn't have to have symptoms.

00:03:28.800 --> 00:03:31.730
You have it even though you say you never felt better.

00:03:32.400 --> 00:03:36.200
[Rodney:] What's that mean? Am I a goner?
I'm too young to die. 

00:03:36.600 --> 00:03:41.400
People my age don't get TB. It must be something else. 
Maybe it's just a touch of asthma.

00:03:41.400 --> 00:03:42.800
Don't you think it's asthma, doc?

00:03:43.600 --> 00:03:48.600
[Doctor:] Slow down, son.
People any age can get tuberculosis.

00:03:49.000 --> 00:03:52.000
Because you've had regular physical checkups,

00:03:52.200 --> 00:03:56.100
we found your TB before it had a chance to get really bad.

00:03:57.000 --> 00:04:00.000
Early tuberculosis like yours can be cured.

00:04:00.800 --> 00:04:02.800
Sure, it takes a little time.

00:04:03.400 --> 00:04:05.200
[Rodney:] Say, what is TB anyway?

00:04:05.800 --> 00:04:10.800
[Doctor:] Well, it's a disease caused by the tubercle bacillus,
a germ. 

00:04:11.200 --> 00:04:13.500
These germs get into the air around us.

00:04:15.400 --> 00:04:22.400
They can stay alive in the air all right, but they'd 
rather be in a place where it's warm and moist and dark,

00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:29.000
like your lungs,
where they can multiply and grow and get plenty of food.

00:04:29.400 --> 00:04:31.000
[Rodney:] Food, me?

00:04:31.200 --> 00:04:38.620
[Doctor:] Yes, you, you're their food.
If the germs get into your lungs and your body

00:04:38.620 --> 00:04:44.400
doesn't fight back fast enough, then you get
active TB and you can spread germs.

00:04:45.500 --> 00:04:49.800
[Rodney:] How about that?
Well who spread it to me? How'd I get it?

00:04:49.879 --> 00:04:54.810
[Doctor:] Well, there's no knowing exactly.
Maybe you've been around people who had TB

00:04:54.810 --> 00:05:00.759
and didn't know it. They're spitting, sneezing,
and coughing can load the air with TB germs.

00:05:00.759 --> 00:05:05.700
[Rodney:] Gee, I always thought TB was something
found only in places like slums.

00:05:05.700 --> 00:05:10.600
[Doctor:] Sure, in any crowded place disease
can spread faster, to more people,

00:05:11.300 --> 00:05:17.200
and these people may not be able to get enough
good food, rest, and medical checkups.

00:05:17.400 --> 00:05:24.400
But Rodney, germs don't know one person from another.
Rich or poor, little or big,

00:05:25.000 --> 00:05:26.600
anyone can get TB.

00:05:29.600 --> 00:05:32.600
[Rodney:] I guess coughing, sneezing,
or spitting is bad anywhere.

00:05:33.200 --> 00:05:40.000
[Doctor:] It sure is, but everyone has the
same good chance to be cured if TB is found early,

00:05:40.200 --> 00:05:41.840
as early as yours.

00:05:41.840 --> 00:05:46.009
[Rodney:] Okay, how do we start curing it?
What sort of medicine do I take?

00:05:46.009 --> 00:05:49.860
[Doctor:] The best medicine for you is plenty of rest.

00:05:49.860 --> 00:05:53.800
[Rodney:] I'll start today. Afternoon naps?

00:05:53.800 --> 00:05:59.430
[Doctor:] No, the best place to rest is in
a tuberculosis hospital.

00:05:59.430 --> 00:06:04.800
[Rodney:] Hospital? But I can't go way off somewhere.
I've never even been out of the state.

00:06:04.800 --> 00:06:08.800
I'd rather rest at home. 
Besides, I don't want to go.

00:06:08.800 --> 00:06:13.000
[Music]

00:06:13.000 --> 00:06:18.070
[Narrator:] So, Rodney entered the hospital,
but it wasn't up in the mountains

00:06:18.400 --> 00:06:20.200
and it wasn't off in a desert.

00:06:20.400 --> 00:06:30.000
[Zoom in on large white building in a bucolic setting
with music playing.]

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:31.400
[Rodney:] This where I'm going to stay, doc?

00:06:31.600 --> 00:06:32.600
[Doctor:] That's right.

00:06:33.350 --> 00:06:36.520
[Rodney:] Right here near home? Well this isn't so bad.

00:06:36.520 --> 00:06:42.200
[Doctor:] Of course not, Rod.
What's going to get you well is good food and rest, 

00:06:42.600 --> 00:06:50.770
mostly rest. You don't have to go to a special planet.
We can cure TB in any planet, because you

00:06:50.770 --> 00:06:58.600
can get plenty of rest and the proper food in
any tuberculosis hospital, wherever it is.

00:06:59.600 --> 00:07:03.600
[Rodney:] Gee, my folks can come here to see me...
and my girl.

00:07:04.000 --> 00:07:06.000
Hey, you're sure they don't have it?

00:07:06.470 --> 00:07:11.320
[Doctor:] All the tests we've made show your
folks are okay, Rod. And we'll keep further

00:07:11.320 --> 00:07:16.620
check on them to make sure they stay that way.
That's another reason for coming here, Rod.

00:07:16.620 --> 00:07:19.600
It protects your family and friends.

00:07:21.600 --> 00:07:24.200
[Rodney:] Say Doc, what do I do to keep in
shape around here?

00:07:24.400 --> 00:07:26.600
I can take a good workout once in a while, can't I?

00:07:27.000 --> 00:07:31.400
[Doctor:] Son, you're sick.
Look, if you had a broken foot,

00:07:31.600 --> 00:07:34.600
do you think it would be smart to kick a football with it?

00:07:35.800 --> 00:07:39.800
If your back was sprained, would you mow the lawn?

00:07:40.600 --> 00:07:45.200
It's the same with your lungs,
you want to keep them as quiet as possible

00:07:45.400 --> 00:07:46.000
so they'll heal.

00:07:47.400 --> 00:07:53.400
Do you know that just running takes fifty
breaths a minute? Even walking takes twenty-five.

00:07:55.800 --> 00:08:01.200
You stay in bed 
and you'll be using your lungs as little as possible.

00:08:02.800 --> 00:08:07.600
Sometimes when a lung can't heal,
even with complete bed rest,

00:08:08.200 --> 00:08:12.400
an operation can collapse it so it doesn't have to do any work at all.

00:08:13.200 --> 00:08:18.400
The other lung does all the breathing necessary
until the diseased lung heals

00:08:18.600 --> 00:08:20.600
and can go back to work again.

00:08:21.200 --> 00:08:27.600
But we're not going to try that now. 
You can see why we want you here instead of at home.

00:08:27.700 --> 00:08:32.000
Home just isn't designed for the kind of rest you need.

00:08:32.000 --> 00:08:36.800
[Music]

00:08:36.800 --> 00:08:42.000
This hospital is. And if you should need drugs or surgery,

00:08:42.400 --> 00:08:46.800
the hospital doctors and nurses are right here to give them to you.

00:08:46.800 --> 00:08:51.800
[Music]

00:08:51.800 --> 00:08:53.200
[Narrator:] Rodney caught on.

00:08:55.000 --> 00:08:59.400
The thing that would cure him now was not activity but rest.

00:09:00.200 --> 00:09:06.000
The medicine was rest,
good food and plenty of rest.

00:09:07.000 --> 00:09:11.800
It didn't happen overnight, 
but the rest began to do its job.

00:09:12.800 --> 00:09:16.600
Gradually his lungs began to heal,
to get well.

00:09:17.800 --> 00:09:23.200
He could use them more and more
until finally, one day...

00:09:23.600 --> 00:09:29.800
[Rodney strides down Main Street with his girl.]

00:09:30.800 --> 00:09:33.200
[Guy:] Hey, Rod's back. Hi ya, Rod.

00:09:34.000 --> 00:09:34.800
[Rodney:] Hi ya.

00:09:36.600 --> 00:09:38.000
[Guy #2:] Gee, Rod was lucky.

00:09:39.000 --> 00:09:40.600
[Narrator:] Lucky, no.

00:09:41.800 --> 00:09:43.400
Rodney was smart,

00:09:44.400 --> 00:09:46.600
smart enough to have a medical checkup

00:09:47.000 --> 00:09:50.600
and an x-ray once a year.

00:09:50.600 --> 00:10:01.800
[Music]

00:10:01.800 --> 00:10:14.400
[The End. National Tuberculosis Association.
Copyright 1950.]

